    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `label_ticket` in `app/labeler.py`, and `pytest -q` passes (1 test). That test only checks that the labels are lowercase, so nothing has actually called `label_ticket` against the API or a fake client yet.\n\nHow it works:\n- It sends one `client.messages.create` request with `model=MODEL`, `max_tokens=5`, `temperature=0`, the ticket text as the user message, and this system prompt: *\"Classify the support ticket. Answer with exactly one word from this list: bug, feature, question.\"* The prompt is built from `LABELS`, so it stays in sync if the labels change.\n- It joins the text of the reply, strips and lower-cases it, and returns it if it's in `LABELS`. Anything else returns `\"question\"`.\n- No dependencies were added.\n\n`temperature=0` works on the current `MODEL` (`claude-sonnet-4-6`), but newer Claude models (Sonnet 5, Opus 4.7 and later, Fable) reject `temperature` with a 400 error. If `MODEL` is ever changed to one of those, every request from `label_ticket` will fail. `app/summaries.py` also uses `MODEL`, so upgrading it for the summaries would break the labeler too. You'd then need to drop `temperature` and rely on the fallback to `\"question\"` instead."
